fact WorkItem(id: "WI-896", description: some(value: "A LABELED PREDICATE RULE'S HEAD FUNCTOR STILL TRAVELS TO THE GLOBAL NAMESPACE — the one shape WI-894 left unscoped, and the spec says so explicitly (`docs/kernel-language.md` §'The label rule is a frozen boundary, not a semantics').\nTHE ASYMMETRY, MEASURED: `rule gte(?x, 3.0) :- gte(?x, 5.0)` (unlabeled) mints a scope-local `<ns>.gte`; the LABELED twin `rule bound: gte(...) :- gte(...)` mints nothing and rides the bare global name. Same head, same meaning, opposite scoping — decided by whether a label is present.\nWHY WI-894 FROZE IT RATHER THAN FIXING IT: removing the label carve-out was tried and BROKE 9 TARGETS (every smt-gen policy render, the whole `prove_*` family). Those fixtures write `rule bound: gte(?x, 3.0) :- gte(?x, 5.0)` as a LEMMA ABOUT the prelude's `gte`, and minting a local `<ns>.gte` silently changes what the lemma is about — so the carve-out is load-bearing, not cosmetic.\nWHY THE OBVIOUS UNIFICATION DOES NOT WORK, checked before filing: gating the predicate path on `implicit_qualified` (the discriminator WI-894 uses for the EQUATION path, where it is correct) re-triggers WI-523 — a user's own `or`/`not` predicate head must keep its own Goal symbol rather than rerouting to the kernel primitive — and `gte` is a prelude name too, so it lands on the same side as `or`. The available refinement is a TWO-LEVEL split, `kernel_vocab_qualified` (never introducible) vs `prelude_qualified` (introducible), which does not by itself settle `gte`.\nSO THE REAL QUESTION IS A LANGUAGE ONE, and it is what this ticket must answer: when a predicate rule head names something no scope defines, is it DEFINING a new predicate or CONCLUDING about an existing one — and what in the source says which? Today the answer is 'the presence of a label', which is not a reason. ACCEPTANCE: the four cases (labeled/unlabeled x new-name/prelude-name) are each decided by a stated rule rather than by the label; the 9 targets above still pass; full workspace green. REFERENCE: WI-894 (which scoped the equation half and froze this one), WI-530 / WI-523 (the implicit-prelude decision and the regression a broad skip caused)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-31T14:37:30Z"))
